WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:09.832
- You know, I think I have to start with some congratulations that are in order.

00:00:09.832 --> 00:00:13.299
I'm hearing that you just signed with Gatorade.

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:15.899
I mean, how does it feel to just hear that?

00:00:16.932 --> 00:00:17.565
- It's crazy.

00:00:17.632 --> 00:00:18.365
It's surreal.

00:00:18.399 --> 00:00:19.532
I'm super excited.

00:00:20.165 --> 00:00:25.532
Gatorade's like the sports leader in
fueling, and they have plenty of household names.

00:00:25.532 --> 00:00:27.865
So to be part of that family is crazy.

00:00:27.865 --> 00:00:34.831
- Yeah, because I would assume, I mean, you grew up drinking
Gatorade all the time, and now you're totally a part of the family.

00:00:34.831 --> 00:00:39.264
I mean, did little Paige ever think, "Hey, I'm gonna partner with these people one day"?

00:00:39.264 --> 00:00:40.564
- Absolutely not.

00:00:40.564 --> 00:00:43.431
I was just thinking, "Man, I need a Gatorade," every time I played basketball.

00:00:43.431 --> 00:00:50.097
Every time at the park, every time after a workout, I—that
was my drink, but I never thought I would be a part of that.

00:00:50.097 --> 00:00:57.430
- Can we both agree that, like, Glacier Freeze is the best flavor,
followed by Cool Blue? But Glacier Freeze—

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.930
- Glacier, Glacier Cherry. That's—

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:03.363
Glacier Freeze is good. Glacier Cherry is my favorite for sure.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.796
- Well, now it's time to see if you're good at Heads Up,

00:01:07.196 --> 00:01:07.529
OK?

00:01:08.063 --> 00:01:13.562
Do you wanna do movies, TV, basketball or superstars?

00:01:13.562 --> 00:01:14.362
- Basketball

00:01:15.829 --> 00:01:17.662
- Uh, Damian Lillard's team.

00:01:17.662 --> 00:01:19.029
- Trail Blazers.

00:01:19.395 --> 00:01:20.229
- Yep.

00:01:22.162 --> 00:01:27.728
Um, this—he played with Kobe. He is very close with Vanessa.

00:01:27.728 --> 00:01:28.962
- Pau Gasol.

00:01:28.962 --> 00:01:30.628
- Yes.

00:01:31.395 --> 00:01:38.494
Oh, this is a movie about a non-Black dude who can play basketball.

00:01:38.494 --> 00:01:40.128
- "White Men Can't Jump"?

00:01:40.128 --> 00:01:41.794
- Yes. [Laughing]

00:01:42.694 --> 00:01:44.994
This is, um, Pistol "blank."

00:01:44.994 --> 00:01:46.827
He has, like, videos that—
- Pistol Pete?

00:01:46.827 --> 00:01:47.027
- Yeah,

00:01:47.027 --> 00:01:48.527
but—yes, Pistol Pete. That's it,

00:01:48.761 --> 00:01:48.961
yeah.

00:01:49.127 --> 00:01:49.960
But his last name.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:50.960
- Pete Maravich.

00:01:51.094 --> 00:01:51.427
Yes.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:52.127
- OK,

00:01:52.194 --> 00:01:53.127
Toronto Raptor.

00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:53.827
Great dunker.

00:01:54.927 --> 00:01:55.493
- Vince Carter.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.127
- Yes.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.860
This is when you shoot the ball and it goes, like, right in.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:02.493
- Swish?

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.060
- No, it's a phrase for that.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:10.726
It's like, it's like, OK, what is the white thing called around the hoop?

00:02:11.093 --> 00:02:12.493
- The net. Nothing but net.

00:02:12.493 --> 00:02:13.759
- Yes! Yes, yes, yes.

00:02:14.359 --> 00:02:14.926
That was good.

00:02:14.926 --> 00:02:15.192
OK,

00:02:15.192 --> 00:02:17.292
we were good. We were good. [Laughing]

00:02:18.526 --> 00:02:24.825
You want women's sports to be recognized and elevated in a very authentic way.

00:02:25.059 --> 00:02:29.458
Do you truly feel that that shift is happening for women's sports?

00:02:30.125 --> 00:02:30.492
- I do.

00:02:30.492 --> 00:02:35.925
I think, I mean, it's not gonna be something that happens
overnight and over a small period of time. It's gonna

00:02:35.925 --> 00:02:38.591
be something that's gonna be worked on for a long time.

00:02:38.591 --> 00:02:44.058
But I think it's important for people like me who
have huge media platforms in a huge way to do it.

00:02:44.058 --> 00:02:48.424
I think it's important that we put that at the forefront, and that's been huge

00:02:48.424 --> 00:02:50.957
in every one of my deals, every one of my partnerships.

00:02:50.991 --> 00:02:53.490
And I think, I mean, I can see the changes.

00:02:53.524 --> 00:02:55.124
There's a long way to go, obviously.

00:02:55.124 --> 00:02:57.157
But little steps and progress are important.

00:02:57.457 --> 00:02:59.357
- You have been an advocate for

00:02:59.357 --> 00:03:02.490
all groups of people, most notably women and Black women.

00:03:02.823 --> 00:03:07.323
At what point would you say that that became a major priority for you?

00:03:08.523 --> 00:03:12.789
- I think in high school. Growing up right outside of Minneapolis,

00:03:12.856 --> 00:03:16.923
I saw a lot of police brutality in the city and in my state.

00:03:16.923 --> 00:03:23.522
And I saw the way it impacted our communities
and our culture just in the state of Minnesota.

00:03:23.522 --> 00:03:24.989
So, I mean,

00:03:24.989 --> 00:03:30.189
that was something that I wanted to change and just have a positive voice in.

00:03:30.222 --> 00:03:35.355
I mean, I went to marches. I went to a lot of
community-driven things around Black Lives Matter.

00:03:35.355 --> 00:03:41.621
So as I continued in college, me being a white woman, I had a lot of

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:47.188
focus in the media, on ESPN and all of the sports platforms that exist.

00:03:47.221 --> 00:03:50.388
- How was it finding that voice?

00:03:50.388 --> 00:03:51.521
- It was hard.

00:03:51.521 --> 00:03:57.121
It took some, like, challenging conversations because
it's not something that I can speak on from experience.

00:03:57.121 --> 00:04:02.487
So to speak with other Black women and
other Black people in different communities,

00:04:02.487 --> 00:04:07.887
it was challenging to have those conversations, but I
think that's a part of growth and that's a part of change,

00:04:07.887 --> 00:04:12.386
having hard conversations and conversations
you necessarily don't wanna have.

00:04:12.386 --> 00:04:13.586
But I think it's very important.

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:14.586
- All right.

00:04:14.586 --> 00:04:16.286
So these are your custom decks.

00:04:16.286 --> 00:04:16.586
- OK.

00:04:17.653 --> 00:04:20.019
- Uh, this is a basketball magazine.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:21.353
- "Slam" magazine?

00:04:21.353 --> 00:04:22.286
- Yep. Mmhmm.

00:04:23.186 --> 00:04:28.652
OK, slam "blank."
Like, in basketball when you hit the ball—

00:04:28.652 --> 00:04:29.419
- Oh, a slam dunk.

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:30.252
Slam dunk

00:04:30.252 --> 00:04:31.019
- Yes, yes, yes.

00:04:33.052 --> 00:04:34.852
You won this award.

00:04:35.352 --> 00:04:37.318
He is a basketball coach.

00:04:38.185 --> 00:04:39.485
- Wooden Award?

00:04:39.485 --> 00:04:41.185
- Yes, John Wooden. Yep.

00:04:42.518 --> 00:04:46.051
UConn player, plays for the Storm.

00:04:47.251 --> 00:04:48.918
- Breanna Stewart? Sue Bird?
- Yup!

00:04:48.918 --> 00:04:50.984
Yup, Breanna Stewart.

00:04:50.984 --> 00:04:55.451
This player plays for the Nets. I think growing up, he was your favorite player?

00:04:55.451 --> 00:04:56.284
- Kyrie?

00:04:56.284 --> 00:04:56.551
- Yes.

00:04:56.851 --> 00:04:59.051
You're much better than people that we have on here.

00:04:59.051 --> 00:05:00.551
A lotta people aren't very good at that game,

00:05:00.551 --> 00:05:01.350
I must say
- Really?

00:05:02.017 --> 00:05:04.150
- [Laughing]
- That was pretty good. That was pretty good.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.584
- Whenever I bring you up to people, they will say something to the

00:05:07.584 --> 00:05:11.383
effect of, "I've never seen a player like Paige."

00:05:11.383 --> 00:05:14.583
When you hear people say that, what do you think they mean by that?

00:05:14.583 --> 00:05:20.749
- I think the first thing that comes to mind is probably
my confidence and my sort of aura on the court.

00:05:20.749 --> 00:05:22.416
People use the word swag.

00:05:22.549 --> 00:05:29.049
I would say that's an accurate description,
but I think it's just, I think—I don't know.

00:05:29.116 --> 00:05:32.116
It's—I think there's a lotta things that go into that.

00:05:32.116 --> 00:05:34.015
I think my passing ability is one of them.

00:05:34.049 --> 00:05:39.915
I take pride in my, the way I pass, my vision
and then just my ability to do multiple things,

00:05:40.248 --> 00:05:43.115
whether it be score, defend.

00:05:43.115 --> 00:05:48.415
Pass is the, my favorite, but I think first probably comes, like, the aura

00:05:48.415 --> 00:05:50.915
- Yeah. I mean, it's so nice hearing,

00:05:50.915 --> 00:05:53.014
like, a basketball player say they enjoy passing.

00:05:53.048 --> 00:05:54.348
Everybody wants to shoot.

00:05:54.348 --> 00:05:55.314
- Yeah.

00:05:55.314 --> 00:05:59.681
- How did you learn to love passing the ball?

00:05:59.681 --> 00:06:00.947
- I think it was just a young age.

00:06:00.981 --> 00:06:02.847
I think a lot of it is the God-given talent.

00:06:03.047 --> 00:06:09.480
I mean, you can practice shooting. You can do ball-handling. But the
vision and the way you see the court, it's hard to practice that.

00:06:10.414 --> 00:06:17.580
So when, like, I found that I had that gift, the way I see
things sort of before they happen, and I just love doing it.

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:19.080
Everybody loves scoring.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.980
Everybody can score, but passing is probably my favorite thing.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.246
Uh, when you get fouled, you shoot one of these.

00:06:26.246 --> 00:06:27.113
- Free throw.

00:06:27.113 --> 00:06:29.446
- Yeah.

00:06:29.446 --> 00:06:30.679
Where I went to high school.

00:06:32.079 --> 00:06:33.479
- I just read this last night.

00:06:33.812 --> 00:06:35.479
It's in Minnesota...Hopkins!

00:06:35.746 --> 00:06:36.112
- Yes.

00:06:38.312 --> 00:06:40.079
Where I go to college

00:06:40.079 --> 00:06:40.512
- UConn

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:41.445
- Yes.

00:06:43.645 --> 00:06:48.978
It's, like, the best chicken spot. Closed on Sundays.
Not the best—

00:06:48.978 --> 00:06:51.778
- Chick-fil-A. And it is the best.
- [Laughing]

00:06:51.778 --> 00:06:57.878
- "Criminal Minds," what are they?

00:06:57.878 --> 00:07:00.478
Like, the people—
- Agents?

00:07:00.611 --> 00:07:01.078
- Yeah.

00:07:01.111 --> 00:07:02.311
In what department?

00:07:03.644 --> 00:07:04.944
CIA, FBI, FBI.

00:07:04.944 --> 00:07:06.744
- Yeah, yeah.
- FBI agent.

00:07:08.044 --> 00:07:10.844
-The partnership, the drink I just signed with.

00:07:10.844 --> 00:07:11.477
- Gatorade.

00:07:11.477 --> 00:07:12.210
- Yep.

00:07:14.577 --> 00:07:19.010
Point guard for the Storm.

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:22.177
- Sue Bird.
- Yep.

00:07:22.177 --> 00:07:23.810
State I'm from.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.343
- Where who's from?

00:07:25.343 --> 00:07:25.876
- Time's up.

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:29.609
It was Minnesota
- Oh! Oh, dang it! Glitch! OK.

00:07:29.609 --> 00:07:31.376
We did well, you know what?

00:07:31.376 --> 00:07:33.843
You're pretty good at Heads Up, I'll give you that.

00:07:33.843 --> 00:07:35.576
I wanna talk about expectations,

00:07:35.609 --> 00:07:36.042
OK?

00:07:36.742 --> 00:07:38.209
Which one is heavier?

00:07:38.309 --> 00:07:45.409
The expectations that you had coming into UConn as a
freshman or the expectations that you have to follow up

00:07:45.409 --> 00:07:48.408
an amazing freshman year with a better sophomore year?

00:07:50.242 --> 00:07:51.142
- I think now

00:07:52.042 --> 00:07:55.108
that I've shown people sort of what I'm capable of—

00:07:55.175 --> 00:07:58.075
I mean, when you're a freshman, everybody would be like,
"Oh, she's just a freshman."

00:07:58.075 --> 00:08:02.341
But now that I'm a sophomore, I think things are sort of ramped up.

00:08:02.408 --> 00:08:04.241
And last year we didn't win a national championship, and

00:08:04.241 --> 00:08:10.474
I also think that's a huge expectation when people and kids decide
to go to UConn, that if you go to UConn, you gotta win a national

00:08:10.474 --> 00:08:12.841
championship just because that's what the program is all about.

00:08:13.907 --> 00:08:17.807
So I think the expectations are heaviest most now.

00:08:17.807 --> 00:08:22.373
- Do you think that your coach Geno Auriemma is misunderstood by the public in some ways?

00:08:24.573 --> 00:08:25.007
- Yeah,

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.640
Coach is Coach. I mean, you gotta know him.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.073
You gotta know his sense of humor, his personality.

00:08:31.606 --> 00:08:35.606
A lotta people judge the way—like, his strictness, the way he coaches players

00:08:35.606 --> 00:08:39.672
with stuff he says. But he has such a great heart. He's such a great human.

00:08:39.772 --> 00:08:45.739
And honestly, he gets on us and he's hard on us because
he wants to see us win. He wants to see us succeed.

00:08:45.739 --> 00:08:47.072
He doesn't want us to look bad in the media

00:08:48.072 --> 00:08:52.338
and on the court just because he knows how people portray UConn and portray him.

00:08:52.338 --> 00:09:01.338
But, I mean, he wants the best for us, and whether it be yelling
at us, making fun of us, it's all a part of the way he loves us, so.

00:09:01.805 --> 00:09:07.604
- Yeah, his press conferences always have some soundbite that
everyone's discussing

00:09:07.604 --> 00:09:07.904
All right,

00:09:08.038 --> 00:09:10.971
now tell me biggest thing that you need to work on the rest of the season.

00:09:13.104 --> 00:09:14.304
- The biggest thing I need to work on,

00:09:14.304 --> 00:09:21.704
definitely probably my leadership, my ability to use my voice
and just sort of taking ownership of the team and leading them.

00:09:21.837 --> 00:09:23.304
I see things before they happen.

00:09:23.304 --> 00:09:27.403
So being able to share that with my teammates just to make it easier for them

00:09:27.403 --> 00:09:31.836
and me and just helping our coaches coach I think is huge for us.

00:09:32.103 --> 00:09:32.936
- OK, nice.

00:09:32.936 --> 00:09:40.136
Well, Paige, you've signed this big, great deal
with Gatorade. You're talented. You're a leader.

00:09:40.169 --> 00:09:43.069
You're breaking barriers, and you're only 20 years old.

00:09:43.069 --> 00:09:44.836
So with all that said, I mean, what do you want

00:09:45.202 --> 00:09:48.869
a 25-year-old Paige to be doing?

00:09:48.869 --> 00:09:55.002
- Continuing to sorta lead change, and not only in the women's game, but in sports in general

00:09:55.002 --> 00:09:57.102
Just continue to be the positive light.

00:09:57.268 --> 00:10:03.368
Continue to be a great role model and obviously keep
working hard so I can keep succeeding on the court and

00:10:03.401 --> 00:10:06.401
hope to win three national championships by that time.

00:10:06.435 --> 00:10:08.735
And then hopefully going to the league.

00:10:08.735 --> 00:10:11.168
So huge things, huge things.

00:10:11.168 --> 00:10:12.534
- Amazing.

00:10:12.534 --> 00:10:15.068
Well, we will all be watching every single step of the way.

00:10:15.134 --> 00:10:17.267
Congrats on everything you're doing.

00:10:17.267 --> 00:10:18.201
Huge congrats on Gatorade.

00:10:18.401 --> 00:10:22.134
I'm rooting for you, and I wish you the
best of luck rest of the season and beyond.

00:10:22.367 --> 00:10:23.267
- Thank you so much.

00:10:23.467 --> 00:10:24.534
- Yeah, thank you for your time.

